{
  "Version": 2,
  "Name": "Kecebas2017-QW7-TiO2-SiO2-600nm",
  "Source": "M. A. Kecebas, M. P. Menguc, A. Kosar, and K. Sendur, \"Passive radiative cooling design with broadband optical thin-film filters,\" J. Quant. Spectrosc. Radiat. Transf. (2017), article in press, pp. 1-8 (volume not printed in this copy). doi:10.1016/j.jqsrt.2017.03.046 - Sec. 3, p. 5-6, Eq. (6), Fig. 6(d) and Fig. 7: '7 periodic layers of TiO2 as a high and SiO2 as a low index layer whose thicknesses are determined based on Eq. (6)' around 600 nm",
  "Notes": "Quarter-wave TiO2/SiO2 periodic reflector (7 layers) centred at 600 nm, the building block of the Ag-free periodic radiative-cooling design\nSpecification: High-reflection zone around 600 nm.\nReported: Fig. 7 (read off the plot, approximate): R ~0.98-0.99 from ~520 to ~720 nm, half-maximum edges near ~490 nm and ~790 nm, minimum R ~0.02 near 850 nm, R ~0.35-0.37 at 950-1000 nm, side lobes ~0.42 at 460 nm and ~0.22 at 400 nm.\nNotes: HLHLHLH with the high index (TiO2) next to air, following Fig. 6(d) (Air / High / Low / ... / Substrate). The substrate is not stated for this design; SiO2 (glass-like) is assumed. Eq. (6) sets the optical thickness to pi/2 at lambda0 = 600 nm (quarter wave, normal incidence). TiO2 and SiO2 indices are not given; database dispersions used, so the band width will depend on the TiO2 model (the paper's anatase index is unknown).\nLayers from Sec. 3, p. 5-6, Eq. (6), Fig. 6(d) and Fig. 7: '7 periodic layers of TiO2 as a high and SiO2 as a low index layer whose thicknesses are determined based on Eq. (6)' around 600 nm; layer 1 faces the incident medium.\nNot checked: the paper reports this design only as plots.",
